/-!
# `Iⁿ G` is of finite type, and coherent when `G` is coherent

Let `I` be a sheaf of ideals and `G` a module on the small étale site of an algebraic space `F`.
If `I` and `G` are of finite type then so is the submodule `Iⁿ G`, and consequently `Iⁿ G` is
coherent whenever `G` is coherent and `F` is locally Noetherian.  On a locally Noetherian space
a *quasi-coherent* sheaf of ideals is automatically of finite type, so the finite-type hypothesis
on `I` is not one a consumer has to supply.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Sites.isFiniteType_idealSmul` and `Stacks.Sites.isFiniteType_idealPowSmul`: on an
  arbitrary site, `I · K` and `Iⁿ · K` are of finite type when `I` and `K` are.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteType_idealPowSubmodule`: the same for `Iⁿ G` on an algebraic
  space.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_idealSheaf`: on a locally Noetherian algebraic space a
  quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals is coherent, hence of finite type.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_idealPowModule`: `Iⁿ G` is coherent when `G` is coherent
  and `I` is of finite type, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_idealPowModule_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`: the same with
  quasi-coherence of `I` in place of finite type, which is the shape consumers want.

## Implementation notes

**The induction is on the exponent, not on monomials.**  One might generate `Iⁿ G` by the
products of the degree-`n` monomials in a generating family of `I` with a generating family of
`G`; that bookkeeping is avoided entirely by `Stacks.Sites.idealPowSmul_succ`
(`Iⁿ⁺¹ · N = Iⁿ · (I · N)`) together with `Stacks.Sites.idealPowSmul_zero`, which reduce
everything to the case `n = 1`.  Only that case has content, and it is
`Stacks.Sites.isFiniteType_idealSmul`.

Finite type is `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`, which asks for a covering on which the module
is generated by finitely many sections; the plumbing is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_presheaf_of_coversTop`, `generates_submodule`,
`exists_cover_comb_of_bind` and `generates_restrict`.  A section of `I · K` is only *locally* a
combination of products, which is why `Stacks.Sites.exists_comb_of_mem_ringSectionIdeal_smul`
produces a sieve rather than a global expression.

Two coercion points deserve naming.  The sections of a submodule of a presheaf of modules are
definitionally, but not syntactically, sections of the ambient presheaf, and likewise the
sections of a sheaf of ideals are sections of the sheaf of rings; `Stacks.Sites.submoduleVal`
and `Stacks.Sites.idealVal` are those inclusions bundled as additive maps, so that they may be
applied to a finite sum through `map_sum`.  Commutativity of a ring of sections is also not
found by instance search through the `CommRingCat` spelling, so
`Stacks.Sites.sum_comb_mul` takes it as an explicit hypothesis rather than an instance.

Coherence of `Iⁿ G` uses the *submodule* form of Stacks 07UD in its finite-type shape
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherentOnSite_of_mono_of_isFiniteType`), which needs no
quasi-coherence of `Iⁿ G`; that is the reason finite type is the right intermediate statement
here.  The local-Noetherianity `Fact` instance is therefore not used by
`isCoherent_idealPowModule` beyond what `IsCoherent` itself carries.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 07UD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07UD)
